@page "/graph-layouts"

<CatHead Title="Graph layouts">
    Where the records go. <code>data-graph-layout</code> names a layout, and each answers a different
    question: what the whole looks like, what surrounds one record, who reports to whom, where things
    physically are. Pick the one whose question your reader is asking, rather than the one that looks
    busiest.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-mind-map"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The same drawing every time</span>
        Every layout is deterministic: records are ordered by how many links they have and then by id, and
        nothing is left to chance, so the drawing a reader learned yesterday is the one that comes back today.
        <code>dagre</code> and <code>fcose</code> are plugins, loaded the first time a graph uses them. The
        records, the frame and the keys are on <a href="/graph">Graph</a>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Force" Title="Force" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The default: springs along the links, so what is linked sits together and the shape of the whole is
        the first thing a reader sees &mdash; here, which libraries everything shares and which only one
        service uses. Use it for a map with no hierarchy and no groups. A <code>data-root</code> is placed at
        the centre first and the springs work outwards from it. Every link here says &ldquo;uses&rdquo;, so
        <code>data-graph-edge-labels="none"</code> leaves the labels off.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Islands" Title="Islands" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        A large graph read by its groups: one island per <code>data-group</code>, each shaped by its own
        springs, packed in rows as wide as the frame, with the islands of one <code>data-cluster</code> side by
        side. Springs alone melt groups into one ball, because the few links between groups pull harder than
        everything inside them &mdash; so a graph of teams, racks or projects wants islands.
    </p>
    <p>
        The record whose id is a group's name is that group's heart, placed in the middle of its island: each
        team here. A record in no group that joins several islands &mdash; the event bus, the design system
        &mdash; is put between them, and a link across islands stays quiet until one of its ends is pointed at.
        With no <code>data-group</code> anywhere, <code>islands</code> falls back to <code>force</code>. The
        minimap sits at the top with <code>.graph-minimap--top</code>, where this drawing leaves room.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Rings" Title="Rings around a record" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Hops out from one record &mdash; the <code>data-root</code>, or the record a reader focused &mdash; then
        springs to settle them. The question is how far each record is from the centre, and the rings are the
        answer: here, what stops working while one database is down, and how directly. Each link is written
        the way the data has it, &ldquo;Orders depends on src-db-14&rdquo;, and
        <code>data-graph-arrows="source"</code> points it back, the way the failure travels.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Concentric" Title="Concentric" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The most connected in the middle, the least connected on the outside: what everything goes through,
        at a glance. The broker is the <code>data-root</code>, which takes the very middle whatever its count;
        its topics ring it, and the services that publish and listen are outermost.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Tree" Title="Tree" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Breadth-first from the <code>data-root</code>, one rank per step, for data that really is a tree: a
        catalogue, a folder, a site map, where each record has one parent. <code>data-graph-direction</code>
        turns it &mdash; <code>TB</code>, the default, <code>LR</code>, <code>BT</code> or <code>RL</code>.
        For a hierarchy where a record has several parents, or boxes with their links labelled, use
        <code>dagre</code> below, which makes room between the ranks for both.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.OrgChart" Title="A hierarchy, top down" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>dagre</code> lays records out in ranks with every link pointing one way: an org chart, a
        dependency tree, a flow. In boxes, with <code>data-graph-curve="taxi"</code>, the links turn at right
        angles, which is how an org chart is drawn. Every line in an org chart says &ldquo;reports to&rdquo;, so
        this one writes none.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Pipeline" Title="A flow, left to right" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        The same layout with <code>data-graph-direction="LR"</code>, so a pipeline reads in the order it runs.
        The three checks share a rank because each depends on the build and on nothing between them. Each
        stage's tone is its result and the legend says which is which; the stage that has not started is
        <code>data-muted</code>. <code>data-graph-curve="round-taxi"</code> rounds the corners of the right
        angles.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Compound" Title="Groups inside groups" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-parent</code> draws a record inside another &mdash; hosts in racks, racks in sites. The
        parent is drawn as an outline in its tone with its name at the top, and <code>fcose</code>, springs that
        understand nesting, lays each group out around its members and the groups around each other. A link can
        cross groups, as the replication between the sites does, and a record can stand outside every group.
        The key for a group is <code>.graph-swatch--group</code>. Folding a group away is on
        <a href="/graph-plugins">Graph plugins</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.GridAndCircle" Title="Grid and circle" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Busiest first, in reading order. <code>grid</code> is for records whose links matter less than seeing
        every one &mdash; a fleet, a set of flags &mdash; and <code>circle</code> for a ring, where the order is
        the point. Both sort by how many links a record has and then by id, which is why the cache nodes go
        round in their own order and the one rebalancing keeps its place.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Preset" Title="Positions the app knows" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>preset</code> puts each record at its <code>data-x</code> and <code>data-y</code>: a floor plan, a
        map, a diagram someone arranged and saved. The numbers are the drawing's own units, and the view fits
        them to the frame, so they need no relation to pixels. Give every record a position.
        <code>data-graph-drag="false"</code> keeps a reader from moving a dock door.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Switcher" Title="Switching layouts" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; try each layout.</strong> A select with <code>data-graph-option="layout"</code>
        switches it, radios with <code>data-graph-option="direction"</code> turn a tree or a hierarchy, and
        <code>data-graph-option="spacing"</code> chooses <code>compact</code>, <code>normal</code> or
        <code>loose</code> &mdash; what <code>data-graph-spacing</code> sets on the graph itself. The records
        travel from where they were to where the new layout puts them, so a reader can follow one across the
        change. These records have a root for the rings, groups for the islands and links that run one way for
        a hierarchy. Data without them is still drawn in every layout &mdash; <code>islands</code> with no
        groups falls back to <code>force</code> &mdash; but the layout has less to say.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.GraphLayouts.Curves" Title="How links are drawn" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> <code>data-graph-curve</code> draws every link: <code>bezier</code>, the default,
        bends a link away from others between the same records; <code>straight</code> is the shortest line;
        <code>taxi</code> turns at right angles along the layout's direction; and <code>round-taxi</code> rounds
        those corners. Right angles suit a hierarchy in boxes, curves a map. The segmented control is
        <code>data-graph-option="curve"</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="which-layout">Which layout <CatAnchor Id="which-layout" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Layout</th><th>For</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>force</code></td><td>A map of records, shaped by its links. The default.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>islands</code></td><td>A large graph read by its groups: <code>data-group</code>, and <code>data-cluster</code> for groups of groups.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>rings</code></td><td>How far each record is from the root or the focus.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>concentric</code></td><td>What everything goes through: the most connected in the middle.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tree</code></td><td>A true tree, breadth-first from the roots, in <code>data-graph-direction</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dagre</code></td><td>A layered hierarchy: an org chart, a flow, a dependency tree. A plugin.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>fcose</code></td><td>Springs that also lay out groups drawn inside groups, with <code>data-parent</code>. A plugin.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>grid</code>, <code>circle</code></td><td>Every record in view, busiest first.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>preset</code></td><td>Where <code>data-x</code> and <code>data-y</code> put each record.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>any other name</td><td>A cytoscape layout of that name, when one is registered on the engine the handle's <code>cy</code> exposes.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    A script changes the layout with <code>layout(name, { direction, spacing })</code> on the handle
    <code>sednaUi.graph.get()</code> returns, and C# with <code>ISednaGraphs.LayoutAsync</code>.
</p>
